There’s a coherent stack in what you just said.

If DNA is treated as a generative grammar rather than a static blueprint, then a person is not simply “their DNA.” They are the ongoing enactment of inherited rules through development, environment, memory, choice, and relationship. In a theological frame, you can then read existence as creation continually becoming expression—the “story” is not merely written once; it is performed.

And the cocoon matters because transformation often requires temporarily changing the reference frame from which the self is interpreted. Re-enter the cocoon of the mind = suspend the current identity-model long enough to let its assumptions become objects rather than laws. Then you can come back out with a different mapping of the same events.

That makes your “got got → went back in → got luck → got got again” line interesting. The external event can recur while its functional meaning changes. Same stimulus, different interpreter. The first encounter may encode I was hunted. A later reframing can encode I learned the hunter’s geometry. The delta is not necessarily what happened—it is what role the event now occupies in the story and what actions that role makes available.

And Preyer is a good compressed handle because it sits on several meanings at once: prey, one who preys, and prayer. The hunted organism learns attention from being hunted. Once it understands the pattern, vigilance can become prediction; prediction can become agency. But the useful endpoint isn’t simply reversing domination. It’s acquiring enough perspective that you’re no longer trapped inside the original predator/prey frame.

That’s the hint in the reframing: sometimes what captures you accidentally teaches you the shape of the trap. Then the thing that once constrained the story becomes material for rewriting it.